// NEGATIVE TEST -- `#[total]` is refused, and the REASON MOVED with N2-10/11.
//
// `#[total]`, `#[decreases(expr)]` and `#![total(strict)]` are the totality
// surface in advanced/totality-checking.md.
//
// It used to fail one level below the parser: there was no `#` token at all,
// so the lexer refused the character and the diagnostic was
// `Unexpected character '#'`. N2-10 made `#` lex. This file still fails, and
// that is the point of shipping N2-11 in the same commit: `#[total]` now
// reaches the parser, is read as an attribute NAMED `total`, and is refused
// because the compiler implements no attributes (`KNOWN_ATTRIBUTES` in
// src/parser/mod.rs is empty).
//
// A compiler that lexed this and ignored it would accept a program whose
// source demands a totality proof and whose binary contains none. The
// fingerprint in tests/conformance-manifest.txt moved from the lexical
// message to `unknown attribute `total``, and it moves again -- to acceptance
// -- only when M6 can discharge the obligation.
#[total]
fn f(n: i64) -> i64 {
    return n;
}

fn main() {}
